t nor  a socialist model operates putin's  russia is something like the living  representation of a modern clique  mercantilism or crony capitalism a model  where large companies receive all kinds  of privileges from the political power  in exchange for supporting that power  and following its guidelines therefore  in this country the relationship between  private wealth and public power is very  close the oligarchs direct and control  strategic sectors for moscow's interests  such as for example energy or defence  [Applause]  now how does this all come about in  practice is this something new from  vladimir putin's russia well the truth  is that it is not  in fact we could say that we are already  in at least the second generation of  russian oligarchs because the first  generation emerged from the chaotic  privatization process that the country  underwent during the 1990s when the  russian state sold off many of the large  public enterprises of the soviet era at  bargain prices it was a process riddled  with corruption that was based on stock  loan schemes a system that used the  shares of large public natural resource  companies as a kind of collateral for  loans to the federal government and  since the federal government did not  repay those loans in a suspiciously  intentional manner then the creditors  who were tycoons and characters close to  power auctioned those shares to recover  their loans auctions that of course were  opaque and rigged where they presented  themselves as the only buyers  and this is how the oligarchs were born  and how they took control of the  country's large companies in this way  the civil servants and public  bureaucrats were replaced by these new  businessmen from socialism we went to  clique mercantilism this whole process  took place during the yeltsin era but in  reality the putin era has not been so  different if anything it's been an  evolution of this system  [Music]  boris yeltsin and vladimir putin were  antagonistic characters who despite  their political ties did not get along  well with each other and of course each  of them had their own groups of  influence which in practice were  adversaries so with putin came the  second generation of oligarchs his  entourage made its way through generous  government contracts concessions and  permits to exploit the country's riches  or supposedly develop national industry  in this way they gradually occupied the  highest rungs of the russian economic  structure  it may surprise you but russia's clinque  mercantilism doesn't work much  differently from organized crime groups  in fact the russian president is a  specialist in getting rid of anyone who  makes him uncomfortable for example by  leading anti-corruption campaigns  take the case of mikhail koldikovsky an  oligarch very close to yeltsin and one  of russia's greatest fortunes who was  imprisoned in 2003 on corruption charges  kodakovsky was the owner of the russian  oil company yukos which was expropriated  by the russian government  police in russia seized oil tycoon  russia's richest man the oil baron  mikhail b cordovsky was seized at  gunpoint on saturday by government  security agents and jailed on charges of  fraud and tax evasion yes all these  accusations may have been true but they  were certainly not exceptional  corruption is a necessary part of the  prevailing political model in the  country this 2003 operation against  russia's largest fortune was putin's  first warning shot to the oligarchs the  message went something like watch out  watch out watch out if you still want to  go out on your own you will suffer the  consequences you are where you are  thanks to the political power so if you  are not completely loyal you will pay  dearly  [Music]  in other words we're not talking here  about businessmen competing with each  other and taking risks not at all the  russian mercantilist model replaces  competition with favored deals influence  peddling bribery and corruption of a