ll  kinds in exchange for their fortunes  that oligarchs undertake to maintain  stability loyalty to political power and  try to run their companies as well as  possible at least better than the  results achieved when the companies were  public but now that we know what exactly  an oligarch is and why they are much  talked about the question is who exactly  are the owners of russia what changes  did vladimir putin bring about how far  does the russian president's influence  over the country's big business extend  well let's take a look  [Music]  russian shareholders  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  the russian oligarchic model as we have  told you has basically gone through two  stages that of yeltsin and that of putin  the first was characterized by the  enormous political power that some of  the oligarchs garnered on some occasions  they even dictated to the government the  direction of its policies or became  members of the cabinet of the government  however in the second stage this  situation changed radically putin  established a very different  relationship with an iron fist the  oligarchs would no longer interfere or  put conditions on the government  russia's power is in the kremlin not in  the headquarters of these companies  [Music]  in this way putin pushed for rules and  laws so that the big oligarchs of  strategic industries such as energy  mining or the media would sell part of  their shares to the state or to entities  controlled by it the idea was to further  tighten the ties between the kremlin and  large russian corporations and this is  how the so-called state corporations  were born which putin legally shielded  with preferential treatment and  privileges of all kinds it is estimated  that these corporations now control 55  of the russian economy it is a variant  of state capitalism that has given putin  unprecedented control over the most  important productive sectors of the  country and also over their owners  that is to say unlike in the yeltsin era  russian oligarchs in practice are no  longer limited to being the owners of  large industrial conglomerates but are  above all responsible for managing these  groups on behalf of the kremlin in this  way the bribes and rents extracted from  these large companies reach all the  levers of power in the country  [Music]  that is the great change that has taken  place and it is what has given vladimir  putin unprecedented power and this is  also what explains that despite all the  international sanctions russia is  suffering after the invasion of ukraine  the oligarchs have barely spoken out  because now they depend on putin more  than putin depends on them of course  this system leads to very poor levels of  productivity innovation and  competitiveness but it does guarantee  enormous stability for all parties  except of course for the vast majority  of russians but having said that there  is an obvious question that arises who  exactly are the country's main oligarchs  [Music]  the friends of the kremlin  [Music]  [Applause]  currently in russia we can find three  types of oligarchs firstly there are  putin's friends who are connected on a  personal level with the president his  inner circle we are talking for example  about former friends from saint  petersburg where the russian president  grew up or former colleagues from the  kgb then there are the leaders of the  security services fsb police and  military who are known as the silo vicky  and finally we have the outsiders well  with regard to this last group these are  people who literally owe putin  everything people who have managed to  multiply their wealth to unimaginable  levels since putin came to power they  are the faithful among the faithful  take the case of yuri kovalchuk known as  putin's personal banker and who has a  master fortune of 1.7 billion he is  currently the main shareholder of rossia  bank one of the largest banks in the  country but if this sounds like a lot of  money check out leonid mikkelsen another  personal friend of the russian  government mikkelsen has a fortune  estimated at 18.1  billion and is currently listed at  number 66 among the richest people in  the world according to forbes mikkelsen  is the founder and director of nova tech  a natural gas company which is currently  sanctioned by the west precisely because  of its ties to the kremlin  then another personal friend of the  president gennady timchenko who has  managed to annas a fortune of 15.7  billion dollars through the vulgar group  his investment company through which he  invests in corporations controlled by  other oligarchs  [Music]  then in the silowiki camp of the  oligarchs who came from the security  services and so on we have the example  of igor sechin who is now president of  the russian oil company rosneft but who  in the 1980s was a soviet spy in  mozambique although officially his  activity was listed as a mere translator  i hope i don't get novochox poisoning  from talking about all these oligarchs  putin understood early on that having  full control of the security services  and gaining the absolute loyalty of  their leaders was a key step in  consolidating his power so he enriched  them beyond measure and then finally in  the outsider group we have a number of  men who managed to save themselves from  putin's purge of oligarchs following the  yeltsin era  one of them is vladimir potanin who  benefited from yeltsin's loan for share  scheme to a massive fortune that totals  16.5 billion today putanen owns 30 of  norelisk nickel a major mining company  as well as stakes in insurance media oil  and a long list of other companies  as you can see in russia fortunes are  not built by entrepreneurial spirit but  above all by contact with the kremlin  and take note because we are not talking  about small change the great russian  billionaires accumulate wealth valued  between 400 and 800  billion dollars yeah billion with a b if  you didn't hear that i said billion  that's a lot of zeros the problem is  that this model makes it possible to  operate basic companies but not to  develop overly complex businesses and  that is exactly one of russia's biggest  problems  the country that has been unable to be  anything more than a mere exporter of  natural resources and then corruption  stifles any possible evolution this is  what explains why russia is one of the  most unequal and least prosperous  countries of course the incentive for  the great russian oligarch is to show  unwavering support to the kremlin to  live the high life and become  ambassadors of moscow in the richest  places in the world of course right now  the sanctions are hitting them hard  [Music]  you see hitting the big oligarchs is  tantamount to hitting the russian state  that is why the allies have targeted  them they are also hoping that  sufficient pressure might even provoke a  shuffling of chairs in moscow after all  it is expected that no one will want to  lose their status all of this explains  why many countries are sanctioning the  oligarchs even more vigorously than  other russian institutions check it out  [Music]  at the moment and as we told you in a  past patreon bulletin russian oligarchs  are seeking refuge in places like dubai  when it comes down to it they are  perhaps the ones who trust putin the  least and by the way if you want to  receive our bulletin the weekly snapshot  just sign up to our patreon community  with your help we can make this project  grow even more and create more and  better independent content in addition  to the weekly snapshot there are options  for receiving a latin politic exclusive  content and unique merchandising we'll  leave you the link in the description  but now it's your turn do you think  sanctions will lead the oligarchs to  betray putin what do you think of this  sort of clique mercantilism that rules  russia should western governments  relentlessly pursue russian oligarchs  leave us your impressions below and now  if you found this video interesting  don't forget to like and subscribe to  visualpolitik all the best and i'll see  you next time  [Music]  you


